---
/**
 * Internals page — how a request is actually routed, end to end.
 *
 * Written because nothing on the site said any of this: not that there are
 * three instances per server, not that each one exits through a different
 * country, and not what changed on 2026-08-22 when the balancer switched
 * from round-robin to consistent-hash affinity. See `internals.ts` for the
 * full content; this component is layout only.
 *
 * Uses `internals` directly, NOT the merged `ui` object: `src/i18n/ui.ts`'s
 * header comment explains why `internals.ts` is kept out of that merge (its
 * `metaTitle`/`title`/`lede` would silently mask `common`'s keys of the same
 * name under a flat spread).
 *
 * REQUEST-PATH DIAGRAM — REPLACED, not tweaked, twice over. Attempt 1 drew a
 * shared trunk forking into a libgen branch and a gitlab branch, as if nginx
 * chose the destination server per request. It does not: a client points
 * its MCP client at mcp.jmrp.io/libgen OR at mcp.jmrp.io/gitlab, and that
 * choice — made on the client, before the request exists on the wire — is
 * what selects the server (two separate `location` blocks in the vhost,
 * each with its own `upstream`; see `docs/nginx-vhost-reference.conf`).
 * Drawing that as a fork taught content-based routing, which is exactly the
 * one thing that is not happening. Attempt 2 fixed that (one chain, played
 * twice in a loop to contrast the IP-keyed and token-keyed passes) but was
 * still, in the author's words, "an animation of dots travelling" rather
 * than an infographic, and did not read well on narrow screens.
 *
 * This build is neither: a compact horizontal strip (client → Cloudflare →
 * nginx → one of N nodes → egress → destination) with a set of six
 * numbered annotation bubbles below it, one at a time, each pointing at the
 * strip piece it describes (see the approved spec at
 * `.superpowers/sdd/internals-diagram-spec.md` for the exact bubble
 * sequence and the non-negotiables). The bubble area reserves a FIXED
 * height (the tallest bubble's) so the page never jumps as they cycle —
 * that fixed reservation is the whole point: it is what "an infographic",
 * not "an animation", means in CSS terms here.
 *
 * UPDATE 2026-08-23, after the author's live review of the first cut: the
 * strip itself now carries ONE highlight indicator (`.mcp-box-fill` /
 * `.mcp-box-border`, see the "Traveling highlight box" section of the
 * <style> block) that glides continuously along it, dwelling at whichever
 * stage the current bubble narrates and travelling to the next with an
 * accelerate/cruise/decelerate profile — never appearing or disappearing.
 * The first cut instead highlighted each stage with its own opacity-only
 * `::after` overlay, which the author read as "tosco" (choppy): the
 * highlight popped in at one stage and popped out at the next instead of
 * being seen to move. This is NOT a reversion to the "dots travelling"
 * design rejected in Attempt 2 above — there is still exactly one
 * indicator, not a race of particles, and the bubbles below (not the
 * indicator) still carry the reading order. Both new elements share the
 * exact same `box-travel` animation and the bubbles' `phase-N` keyframes
 * share its timeline, so the indicator always arrives exactly when its
 * bubble's text appears.
 *
 * UPDATE 2026-08-23 (destination hop): the strip always had six stages
 * (client → Cloudflare → nginx → nodes → egress → destination — see "The
 * strip" section of the <style> block), but only the first five had a
 * bubble/timeline step/box dwell, so the traveling box jumped straight from
 * "egress" back to "client" and the caption never mentioned that egress
 * hands off to an actual destination. The author: "añade punto 6 que es la
 * salida, indicando que la petición sale hacia el objetivo que se le ha
 * indicado al mcp... así la caja pasa por el último punto." Bubble 6 /
 * `diagramTimelineStep6` narrate that hand-off (a Library Genesis mirror
 * for libgen, or the GitLab instance the client pointed gitlab at — the
 * same wording `pathBody` already used), `box-travel` gained a sixth dwell
 * plus a fifth short forward hop (egress → destination), and the closing
 * RETURN hop now runs destination → client instead of egress → client. The
 * three-segment ease-in/linear/ease-out shape of every hop, and the "box
 * never disappears" invariant, are unchanged — see "Bubble cycle" and
 * "box-travel" below for the new timing table.
 *
 * The bubbles describe the mechanism in general terms (with a token, or
 * without one) rather than singling out libgen vs gitlab or which specific
 * node a given request lands on — see `topology.ts`'s header comment for
 * why the node pool this diagram draws is `topology.libgen` alone, and the
 * numbered prose timeline right below the figure for the full detail, in
 * real HTML, that the bubbles deliberately keep short.
 */
import type { McpInstance } from "../../data/topology";
import { topology } from "../../data/topology";
import { ui } from "../../i18n/ui";
import { internals } from "../../i18n/ui/internals.ts";
import { getLangFromUrl } from "../../i18n/utils";
import { pageUrl } from "../../lib/seo";
import UpdatedLine from "../UpdatedLine.astro";

const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const t = internals[lang];
// `backToHome` is shared chrome, so it lives in `common` and reaches this
// page through `ui` — `internals` is deliberately outside that merge.
const shared = ui[lang];
// Site-relative, like every other cross-page link on this site: an absolute
// URL here would jump a dev or preview server straight to production.
const homeHref = new URL(pageUrl(lang, "home")).pathname;
const policiesHref = new URL(pageUrl(lang, "policies")).pathname;

// The nginx directive that actually runs in production, quoted whole so a
// visitor can check the claims in the surrounding prose against real code —
// EXCEPT the salt itself, which is not a value this page can show.
//
// SECURITY: `"…"` stands in for the salt (48 hex characters), which lives
// only in `secrets/affinity-salt.env` on the server and is never checked
// into this repo or built into this site. Do not replace this placeholder
// with a real value, here or anywhere else.
//
// SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING: hand-marked tokens, not a highlighter. `astro:
// components`'s `<Code />` (Shiki) was tried first, as the Restriction
// asked — it is zero client JS (Shiki runs at build time) and ships with
// Astro already, so it adds no dependency. It was rejected on two counts,
// both checked against this exact snippet rather than assumed: (1) every
// bundled Shiki theme carries its OWN fixed palette (arbitrary hex, e.g. a
// VS-Code-style blue/orange/green set) with no supported way to route
// token colors through this site's existing `--color-*` tokens, which the
// Restriction requires; dual light/dark theming would additionally need
// its own `--astro-code-light`/`--astro-code-dark` CSS-variable switching
// convention, parallel to and disconnected from `tokens.css`'s established
// `:root`/`prefers-color-scheme`/`[data-theme]` one. (2) Shiki's stock
// "nginx" grammar does not tokenize the embedded Lua body of
// `set_by_lua_block { ... }` as Lua — the one block that most needs
// contrast (the hash computation this whole section is about) would
// render as one flat, unhighlighted span of "other" text. A 9-line
// snippet that is fixed content (never user input, never changes without
// a code edit) does not need a general-purpose grammar: hand-marking the
// dozen tokens that matter is less machinery, not more, and every color
// below is an EXISTING token already relied on elsewhere for AA text
// contrast in both themes (`--color-accent`, `--teal-text`,
// `--color-success`, `--color-info`, `--color-text-muted` — see
// Inspector.css for other consumers of the last four as text). Each line
// is an array of `{ text, cls? }` tokens rendered through Astro's normal
// `{expression}` escaping below — no `set:html`, no manual HTML-escaping.
type SnippetToken = { text: string; cls?: "kw" | "var" | "str" | "com" };
const affinityTokens: SnippetToken[] = [
  { text: "set", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " " },
  { text: "$mcp_affinity_salt", cls: "var" },
  { text: " " },
  { text: '"…"', cls: "str" },
  { text: ";   " },
  { text: t.affinityCodeComment, cls: "com" },
  { text: "\n\n" },
  { text: "set_by_lua_block", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " " },
  { text: "$mcp_affinity", cls: "var" },
  { text: " {\n    " },
  { text: "local", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " token = " },
  { text: "ngx.var.http_private_token", cls: "var" },
  { text: "\n    " },
  { text: "if", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " token " },
  { text: "and", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " token ~= " },
  { text: '""', cls: "str" },
  { text: " " },
  { text: "then", cls: "kw" },
  { text: "\n        " },
  { text: "return", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " ngx.md5(" },
  { text: "ngx.var.mcp_affinity_salt", cls: "var" },
  { text: " .. token)\n    " },
  { text: "end", cls: "kw" },
  { text: "\n    " },
  { text: "return", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " " },
  { text: "ngx.var.remote_addr", cls: "var" },
  { text: " " },
  { text: "or", cls: "kw" },
  { text: " " },
  { text: '""', cls: "str" },
  { text: "\n}" },
];

// ===== Request-path diagram — build-time data, no runtime JS ===============
//
// Everything below runs once, on the server, while Astro renders this page's
// HTML. None of it ships to the browser: the diagram that reaches the client
// is a static strip (plain HTML/CSS, no SVG) plus six annotation bubbles and
// one traveling highlight indicator, all cycled by CSS animations alone (see
// the <style> block). This is what "no JavaScript on this page" (Restriction)
// means in practice — the computation happens, just not where a crawler or
// an assistant would have to run it.

// "How many badges fit legibly", not an infrastructure fact — capped so the
// diagram folds anything beyond it into the dim "+" ghost node instead of
// needing a redraw if the real pool grows.
const MAX_DRAWN_NODES = 3;

// The pool of real nodes this diagram draws — see this file's header
// comment and `topology.ts`'s for why reusing one server's real, verified
// pool here is deliberate: the diagram no longer claims to show which
// server, only the mechanism every server uses.
// if a future instance count exceeds `MAX_DRAWN_NODES`, it renders "+N"
// instead — see the markup below.
const nodes: McpInstance[] = topology.libgen.slice(0, MAX_DRAWN_NODES);

// ===== Fan geometry =========================================================
// The strip stopped being a single row of six beads the moment the author
// asked for what actually happens: nginx does not hand a request to "the
// nodes", it picks ONE of three, and that node leaves through ONE of two
// exits. A straight line through a stage called "nodes" drew a pipeline;
// this draws a fork, the way a git graph does.
//
// Everything below is derived from `topology.libgen`, never hand-drawn: the
// number of nodes sets the fan's spread, and the number of DISTINCT egress
// countries sets how many exits there are and which node joins which. Change
// the topology and the picture changes with it — a hand-drawn fan would keep
// claiming the old split. The countries themselves are NOT painted (the
// author: "no hace falta indicar país en la animación, solo son dos
// egress"); they only decide which node shares an exit with which.
//
// Coordinates live in the SVG's own space: x as 0–100 across the strip (so it
// stretches with the grid), y in the px of a track every column shares.
const TRACK_H = 74;
/** Column centers as percentages, for grid tracks of 1 1.5 1 1 1 1.5 (=7). */
const COL_X = [7.143, 25, 42.857, 57.143, 71.429, 89.286];
const MID_Y = TRACK_H / 2;

/** Vertical center of node `i`, spread evenly over the shared track. */
const nodeY = (i: number): number =>
  Math.round((((i + 0.5) / nodes.length) * TRACK_H + Number.EPSILON) * 10) / 10;

// The exits, in first-seen order, and which one each node uses. Two today
// (two countries), and the code never assumes that number.
const egressKeys = [...new Set(nodes.map((n) => n.egressCountry))];
const EGRESS_GAP = 26;
/** Vertical center of exit `i`, centred as a group on the same track. */
const egressY = (i: number): number =>
  MID_Y + (i - (egressKeys.length - 1) / 2) * EGRESS_GAP;

/**
 * One connector, as a cubic with horizontal tangents at both ends — the
 * shape a git graph uses to fork a branch off a trunk. A hop that does not
 * change row stays a straight line: a curve there would only add motion the
 * data does not have.
 *
 * @param x1 Start x (0–100). @param y1 Start y (px in the track).
 * @param x2 End x. @param y2 End y.
 * @returns The `d` attribute.
 */
function wire(x1: number, y1: number, x2: number, y2: number): string {
  if (y1 === y2) return `M ${x1},${y1} L ${x2},${y2}`;
  const dx = x2 - x1;
  return `M ${x1},${y1} C ${x1 + dx * 0.45},${y1} ${x1 + dx * 0.55},${y2} ${x2},${y2}`;
}

/** The four hops, each with the transport that colours it. */
const wires = {
  // you → Cloudflare → nginx: one straight run, TLS end to end.
  tls_in: [wire(COL_X[0], MID_Y, COL_X[2], MID_Y)],
  // nginx → each node: the fork. Loopback, never on a network.
  local: nodes.map((_, i) => wire(COL_X[2], MID_Y, COL_X[3], nodeY(i))),
  // each node → its exit: the join. Inside the SSH tunnel.
  ssh: nodes.map((node, i) =>
    wire(COL_X[3], nodeY(i), COL_X[4], egressY(egressKeys.indexOf(node.egressCountry))),
  ),
  // each exit → the destination: TLS again, and STRAIGHT. An earlier cut
  // curved both exits into a single bead, which drew a claim the routing
  // never makes: that the two exits share a destination. They do not — each
  // request goes wherever its caller pointed it, so the destination is a
  // region, not a host, and every exit enters it on its own line.
  tls_out: egressKeys.map((_, i) => wire(COL_X[4], egressY(i), COL_X[5], egressY(i))),
};

// The node the reduced-motion frozen frame rings as "the chosen one" (see
// the `prefers-reduced-motion` rules in the <style> block). ILLUSTRATIVE,
// not the real affinity outcome for any actual client — computing that
// needs `$mcp_affinity_salt`, the secret this page never has (see
// `affinityTokens` above). Any index works; the point the ring makes is
// "always this same one", not "specifically this one".
const MATCHED_NODE_INDEX = 0;

---

<p class="back-link">
  <a href={homeHref}>{shared.backToHome}</a>
</p>

<h1 class="subpage-title">{t.title}</h1>

<p class="internals-intro">{t.lede}</p>

<section aria-labelledby="path-h">
  <h2 class="eyebrow" id="path-h">{t.pathEyebrow}</h2>
  {t.pathBody.map((paragraph) => <p class="prose-text">{paragraph}</p>)}

  {/* Request-path figure: a strip of six fixed stages (the "aria-hidden"
      icons are pure decoration, the stage names and node numbers/countries
      are real HTML, not SVG text) plus a single highlight indicator that
      glides between all six stages the bubbles narrate, and six
      annotation bubbles that cycle one at a time via CSS animation alone.
      The bubble area's height is reserved by `.mcp-bubbles`'s fixed
      `height` (see the <style> block) — that is what keeps the page from
      jumping as they cycle. `MATCHED_NODE_INDEX` only matters for the
      reduced-motion frozen frame, defined entirely inside that media query
      below. */}
  <figure class="mcp-flow-figure">
    <div class="mcp-strip">
      {/* Traveling highlight box — see the <style> block's "Traveling
          highlight box" section. Two elements sharing one animation: the
          fill goes FIRST in the DOM (paints behind the stages below, via
          `isolation: isolate` on `.mcp-strip` plus its own negative
          `z-index`) and the border goes LAST (paints above everything, via
          a positive `z-index`) — that split is what keeps the highlight's
          background from ever covering a stage's icon or label text. Both
          `aria-hidden`: purely decorative, like every icon on this strip. */}
      <span class="mcp-box-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>

      {/* The connectors, painted BEHIND every stage (see `.mcp-wires` in the
          <style> block) so the opaque icons read as beads on the wires. One
          path per hop, coloured by transport — the flat line this replaced
          could only ever draw a pipeline, and what happens here is a fork:
          nginx picks ONE node, that node leaves through ONE exit. Decorative
          (`aria-hidden`): the six stage names, the node numbers and the prose
          timeline below carry all of it as real text.

          `preserveAspectRatio="none"` lets x stretch with the grid while y
          stays in px, and `vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"` keeps that
          stretch from thickening the strokes. */}
      <svg
        class="mcp-wires"
        viewBox={`0 0 100 ${TRACK_H}`}
        preserveAspectRatio="none"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
        {wires.tls_in.map((d) => <path class="wire wire--tls" d={d} vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />)}
        {wires.local.map((d) => <path class="wire wire--local" d={d} vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />)}
        {wires.ssh.map((d) => <path class="wire wire--ssh" d={d} vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />)}
        {wires.tls_out.map((d) => <path class="wire wire--tls" d={d} vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />)}
      </svg>


      <div class="stage stage--client">
        <span class="stage-icon stage-icon--client" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span><span></span><span></span>
        </span>
        <span class="stage-label">{t.diagramStageClient}</span>
      </div>

      <div class="stage stage--cloudflare">
        <span class="stage-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="stage-label">Cloudflare</span>
      </div>

      <div class="stage stage--nginx">
        <span class="stage-icon stage-icon--nginx" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="stage-label">nginx</span>
      </div>

      <div class="stage stage--nodes">
        <div class="node-cluster">
          {
            nodes.map((_, i) => (
              <span class={`node-badge${i === MATCHED_NODE_INDEX ? " node-badge--matched" : ""}`}>
                <span class="node-badge-num">{i + 1}</span>
              </span>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <span class="stage-label">{t.diagramStageNodes}</span>
      </div>

      <div class="stage stage--egress">
        <div class="egress-stack">
          {egressKeys.map(() => <span class="stage-icon" aria-hidden="true" />)}
        </div>
        <span class="stage-label">{t.diagramStageEgress}</span>
      </div>

      <div class="stage stage--destination">
        <span class="stage-icon stage-icon--zone" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="stage-label">{t.diagramStageDestination}</span>
      </div>

      <span class="mcp-box-border" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>

    {/* Transport legend. The connector line under the strip is painted in
        one colour per transport (see "Connector line" in the <style>
        block); this is what those colours say, in real text — colour alone
        never carries the meaning. It sits between the strip and the bubbles
        because that is where the author asked for it, and because a reader
        meets the coloured line first and the question ("what do the colours
        mean?") immediately after. */}
    <ul class="mcp-legend">
      <li><span class="mcp-legend-swatch mcp-legend-swatch--tls" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.diagramLegendTls}</li>
      <li><span class="mcp-legend-swatch mcp-legend-swatch--local" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.diagramLegendLocal}</li>
      <li><span class="mcp-legend-swatch mcp-legend-swatch--ssh" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.diagramLegendSsh}</li>
    </ul>

    {/* Fixed-height annotation zone: exactly one bubble opaque at a time,
        via the `phase-N` keyframes shared with the highlight box's
        `box-travel` animation above (see the <style> block's "Bubble
        cycle" section for the timing table both share). `bubble-leader`'s
        `left` and the highlight box's travel target both key off the same
        six column positions, defined once as `--pos` per `.bubble--N` and
        reused by its leader/tail — see the <style> block's "Shared column
        positions" comment for why. */}
    <div class="mcp-bubbles">
      <div class="bubble bubble--1">
        <span class="bubble-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="bubble-box"><span class="bubble-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="bubble-text">{t.diagramBubble1}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="bubble bubble--2">
        <span class="bubble-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="bubble-box"><span class="bubble-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="bubble-text">{t.diagramBubble2}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="bubble bubble--3">
        <span class="bubble-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="bubble-box"><span class="bubble-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="bubble-text">{t.diagramBubble3}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="bubble bubble--4">
        <span class="bubble-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="bubble-box"><span class="bubble-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="bubble-text">{t.diagramBubble4}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="bubble bubble--5">
        <span class="bubble-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="bubble-box"><span class="bubble-num" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="bubble-text">{t.diagramBubble5}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="bubble bubble--6">
        <span class="bubble-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="bubble-box"><span class="bubble-num" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span class="bubble-text">{t.diagramBubble6}</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figcaption class="mcp-flow-caption">
      {t.diagramCaption}
      <span class="mcp-legend-note">{t.diagramLegendNote}</span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  {/* Prose timeline: same six numbers as the bubbles above, full detail,
      real HTML — this, not the figure, is what a crawler, an assistant or a
      `prefers-reduced-motion` visitor actually reads. See this file's
      header comment for why the detail lives here and not in the figure. */}
  <p class="prose-text">{t.diagramTimelineIntro}</p>
  <ol class="mcp-timeline">
    <li>
      <span class="mcp-timeline-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
      <p>{t.diagramTimelineStep1}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="mcp-timeline-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
      <p>{t.diagramTimelineStep2}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="mcp-timeline-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
      <p>{t.diagramTimelineStep3}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="mcp-timeline-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
      <p>{t.diagramTimelineStep4}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="mcp-timeline-num" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
      <p>{t.diagramTimelineStep5}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="mcp-timeline-num" aria-hidden="true">6</span>
      <p>{t.diagramTimelineStep6}</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="wire-h">
  <h2 class="eyebrow" id="wire-h">{t.wireEyebrow}</h2>
  {t.wireBody.map((paragraph) => <p class="prose-text">{paragraph}</p>)}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="instances-h">
  <h2 class="eyebrow" id="instances-h">{t.instancesEyebrow}</h2>
  {t.instancesBody.map((paragraph) => <p class="prose-text">{paragraph}</p>)}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="affinity-h">
  <h2 class="eyebrow" id="affinity-h">{t.affinityEyebrow}</h2>
  <p class="prose-text">{t.affinityIntro}</p>
  <p class="prose-text">{t.affinityLibgen}</p>
  <p class="prose-text">{t.affinityGitlab}</p>
  {t.affinityCodeIntro.map((paragraph) => <p class="prose-text">{paragraph}</p>)}
  <pre><code>{affinityTokens.map((tok) =>
    tok.cls ? <span class={`snippet-${tok.cls}`}>{tok.text}</span> : tok.text,
  )}</code></pre>
  <p class="prose-text">{t.affinityConsequence}</p>

</section>

<section aria-labelledby="egress-h">
  <h2 class="eyebrow" id="egress-h">{t.egressEyebrow}</h2>
  {t.egressBody.map((paragraph) => <p class="prose-text">{paragraph}</p>)}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="personal-h">
  <h2 class="eyebrow" id="personal-h">{t.personalEyebrow}</h2>
  {t.personalBody.map((paragraph) => <p class="prose-text">{paragraph}</p>)}
  {/* Points at the full statements (no-SLA, legal position) instead of
      repeating them: this page's job is the mechanism, policies' job is the
      commitment — or lack of one. */}
  <p class="prose-text">
    <a href={policiesHref}>{t.policiesLink}</a>
  </p>
</section>

{/* Visible freshness INSIDE <main>. See `UpdatedLine.astro` for why this
    cannot just live in the footer. */}
<UpdatedLine lang={lang} />

<style>

  /* Heading size lives in base.css's `.subpage-title` now — Inspector and
     Policies already used it; this page had its own hand-copied clamp
     (2.25rem→3.25rem, not 2.125rem→3.25rem) left over from before this page
     could be touched. Dropped in favor of the shared class so all three
     subpages share one rule, per base.css's `.subpage-title` comment. */

  /* Copied from PoliciesPage.astro / InspectorPage.astro's shared rules:
     Astro scopes <style> per component, so a class used on more than one
     page needs its own copy here. */
  .back-link {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .back-link a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back-link a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .internals-intro {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  section {
    padding-block-end: 2.75rem;
  }

  /* Eyebrow with `//`, painted from CSS so it does NOT exist in the DOM —
     `textContent`-based extractors would otherwise read the heading as
     "//The path of a request". See HomePage.astro for the full rationale,
     and for the numbers matched against jmrp.io's `.section-title`: this
     page's `.eyebrow` was the one copy left at the old bare 0.75rem/12px,
     `font-weight: 600` and muted color from before this page could be
     touched — HomePage.astro and PoliciesPage.astro already carry the fixed
     `--text-label` (12.5px) / `--fw-medium` (500) / 0.16em / full accent
     color version. */
  .eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .eyebrow::before {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    content: "//";
  }


  .prose-text a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .prose-text a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* Same treatment as ClientSetup.astro's code blocks: overflow lives INSIDE
     the <pre>, not the page, because main sits in a fixed-width column and a
     long nginx line would otherwise widen it on mobile. */
  pre {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hair);
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--color-bg-subtle);
  }

  pre code {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    white-space: pre;
  }

  /* ===== Snippet token colors ===================================================
     Hand-marked highlighting for the nginx/Lua snippet above — see the
     "SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING" comment on `affinityTokens` in the frontmatter
     for why this is spans + classes instead of a Shiki-powered `<Code />`.
     Four categories, four EXISTING tokens, each already relied on
     elsewhere for AA text contrast in both themes: directive keywords get
     the same accent used on the nginx stage icon/label below (tying the
     snippet back to "where the decision happens"), variables get the
     teal already documented as AA text, strings get the success green,
     and the one translated comment line gets the standard muted/secondary
     text color. Unmarked tokens (braces, dots, the `ngx.md5(...)` call)
     stay the plain `pre code` color above — not every token needs its own
     hue to read clearly. */
  .snippet-kw {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .snippet-var {
    color: var(--teal-text);
  }

  .snippet-str {
    color: var(--color-success);
  }

  .snippet-com {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  /* ===== Request-path diagram =================================================
     A compact horizontal strip (client → Cloudflare → nginx → one of N nodes
     → egress → destination — the six stages themselves never move, resize,
     or reorder) plus six numbered bubbles that cycle one at a time below
     it. See this file's header comment for why: two earlier versions were
     rejected, the second one specifically for still reading as "an
     animation of dots travelling" instead of an infographic, and reserves a
     fixed height for the bubble zone so the page never jumps.

     ONE thing does move now, deliberately: a single highlight indicator
     (`.mcp-box-fill`/`.mcp-box-border`, see the "Traveling highlight box"
     section below) glides between all six stages the bubbles narrate. That
     is not the same defect as "dots travelling" above — there is one
     indicator, not several, it follows the bubbles' reading order rather
     than inventing its own, and the stages/labels/nodes it glides past are
     still fixed HTML in a fixed grid. See this file's header comment,
     "UPDATE 2026-08-23" and "UPDATE 2026-08-23 (destination hop)", for why
     this changed from the original highlight-only version and for why it
     now reaches a sixth stage. */
  /* `--wire-*`: the three transport colours, defined once for the connector
     line and its legend. Site tokens, not new hex — `success` for a hop
     encrypted with TLS, `info` for the SSH tunnel, and the muted text colour
     for the hop that never touches a network (the same neutral this strip
     already uses for structure rather than status). Both themes ship
     AA-contrast values for all three. */
  .mcp-flow-figure {
    /* The shared icon track, in px. Also the SVG's viewBox height, set from
       `TRACK_H` in the frontmatter — the two MUST stay equal or the curves
       stop landing on the icons. */
    --track-h: 74px;
    --wire-tls: var(--color-success);
    --wire-ssh: var(--color-info);
    --wire-local: var(--color-text-muted);

    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
    padding: 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hair);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: var(--color-bg-subtle);
  }

  /* ===== The strip =============================================================
     Six stages on a shared 8-unit grid: client/Cloudflare/nginx/egress/
     destination get 1 column each, nodes gets 3 (it holds a small cluster of
     badges, not one icon). `min-width: 0` on `.stage` is load-bearing — a
     CSS grid track otherwise refuses to shrink a cell below its content's
     natural (unwrapped) width, which for a word like "Cloudflare" or
     "destination" would force the whole strip wider than the viewport at
     360px. With `min-width: 0` plus `overflow-wrap: anywhere` on the label
     below, the grid track wins instead and the word wraps — this is what
     keeps the strip horizontal with zero horizontal scroll at 360px, the
     hard requirement that sank neither, but almost sank, both earlier
     attempts. */
  .mcp-strip {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    /* Cloudflare/destination get a bit more than client/nginx/egress: they
       are the longest words on the strip ("Cloudflare", "destination") and
       would otherwise wrap ugly mid-word even with `overflow-wrap: anywhere`
       below. Nodes still gets by far the most room — see this block's
       header comment. */
    /* The nodes column no longer holds a row of badges (they stack now), so
       it needs no more room than any other; the width goes instead to the two
       longest words, which used to break mid-word at 390px. */
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.5fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1.5fr;
    align-items: start;
    column-gap: 2px;
    /* Gives `.mcp-box-fill`'s negative `z-index` (below) somewhere to land:
       without this, a negative `z-index` on a descendant escapes past
       `.mcp-strip` itself and paints behind `.mcp-flow-figure`'s own
       background instead of just behind the stage icons/labels — see the
       "Traveling highlight box" section below. */
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* The connecting line, painted BEHIND every stage: a `::before` pseudo-
     element is always painted before its element's real children, so the
     opaque icon/badge circles sitting on top of it read as beads on a wire
     with no z-index bookkeeping needed. */
  /* The connectors. Absolutely positioned over the icon track — NOT over the
     whole strip — so the SVG's y axis and the track's px height are the same
     number, which is what lets the frontmatter place a curve's endpoint on a
     node without measuring anything at runtime.

     Behind every stage (`z-index: -1`, with `isolation: isolate` on the strip
     above): the icons are opaque, so the wires disappear under them and the
     stages read as beads threaded on the lines. */
  .mcp-wires {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.2rem; /* the stage's own padding: aligns the track with the icons */
    left: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    width: 100%;
    height: var(--track-h);
  }

  .wire {
    fill: none;
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  .wire--tls {
    stroke: var(--wire-tls);
  }

  .wire--local {
    stroke: var(--wire-local);
  }

  .wire--ssh {
    stroke: var(--wire-ssh);
  }

  /* ===== Transport legend ======================================================
     Three swatches in a row, above the bubbles. The swatch colours are the
     line's own, defined ONCE as `--wire-*` on the figure and consumed by
     both — a legend that can drift from the thing it explains is worse than
     no legend. Colour never carries the meaning alone: each swatch is
     followed by its word, and the `wire*` section below the figure says all
     of it again in prose, which is what a screen reader or a crawler reads.

     This replaced a label-per-joint version (five words riding on the wire).
     It read fine from 600px up and collided with an icon at every hop below
     481px — the gap between two adjacent stage icons is ~30px there and a
     legible label ~27px wide — so the author asked for colour plus one
     horizontal legend instead. */
  .mcp-legend {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.25rem 1rem;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .mcp-legend li {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    align-items: center;
    white-space: nowrap; /* each pair wraps as a unit, never mid-word */
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch {
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 2px;
    border-radius: 1px;
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch--tls {
    background: var(--wire-tls);
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch--local {
    background: var(--wire-local);
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch--ssh {
    background: var(--wire-ssh);
  }

  .mcp-legend-note {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
  }

  /* Two rows: a track every column shares, and the label under it. The track
     is what makes the fan drawable — the SVG's y coordinates are px inside
     exactly this height, so a single icon sits at its middle while three
     stacked nodes spread across it, and both agree with the curves painted
     behind them. `place-items: center` centres whatever the first row holds,
     one icon or a stack of them. */
  .stage {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: var(--track-h) auto;
    place-items: center;
    min-width: 0; /* see this block's header comment */
    padding: 0.2rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  /* ===== Traveling highlight box ===============================================
     ONE indicator that glides continuously along the strip — always
     visible, dwelling at whichever stage the current bubble narrates and
     travelling to the next with an accelerate/cruise/decelerate profile
     (`box-travel` below, shared with the bubbles' timeline — see "Bubble
     cycle" further down). This replaced an earlier version that highlighted
     each stage with its own opacity-only `::after`, popping in at one stage
     and out at the next — the author read that as "tosco" (choppy) and
     asked for the indicator to stay on screen at all times. See this file's
     header comment for the fuller history.

     Two elements, not one — `.mcp-box-fill` and `.mcp-box-border` — because
     CSS cannot give `z-index` to only half of a single element's own box
     (its background, specifically). Splitting it in two is the author's own
     fix for that: a fill-only copy BEHIND the strip's real content (icons,
     labels, node badges — via `isolation: isolate` on `.mcp-strip` above
     plus this element's own negative `z-index`, so the fill can never sit
     on top of a stage's text) and a border-only copy ABOVE everything (a
     positive `z-index`). Both run the exact same `box-travel` animation, so
     they move as a single object with no separate clock to drift out of
     sync.

     `left`/`width` are animated (see `box-travel`); `top`/`height` are NOT
     — every stage's own content height differs ("nodes" is tallest at every
     breakpoint: 52.77px measured at ≥901px, 49.39px at 481–900px, 45.84px
     at ≤480px), and one element cannot re-measure six different DOM boxes
     in pure CSS. Sized generously to the tallest stage at each breakpoint
     instead, so the indicator reads as one consistent "spotlight" gliding
     under icon+label alike rather than tracing each stage's exact outline. */
  .mcp-box-fill,
  .mcp-box-border {
    position: absolute;
    top: -4px;
    /* The shared track plus the label under it, plus 4px of air each side —
       every column is the same height now, so one value fits all six. */
    height: calc(var(--track-h) + 30px);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    /* `linear` here is a harmless default: every dwell segment below holds
       its value constant (no interpolation needed) and every travel segment
       sets its OWN `animation-timing-function` per keyframe — see
       `box-travel`'s header comment. */
    animation: box-travel 30s linear infinite;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .mcp-box-fill {
    z-index: -1; /* behind every stage's static (non-positioned) content */
    background: var(--accent-dim);
  }

  .mcp-box-border {
    z-index: 2; /* above the connecting line and every stage/node badge */
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .stage-icon {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    border: 1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
  }

  /* nginx: where the decision happens, drawn "in focus" even at rest — a
     thicker accent ring and a tinted fill, not just a highlight during its
     own bubble's window. */
  /* The destination is not a host: it is whatever each caller pointed its
     client at, which is why the two exits enter it on separate straight lines
     instead of meeting at a bead. A tall block reads as a region the way a
     circle cannot — and, being opaque like every other icon, it also masks
     the wire ends that stop behind it. */
  .stage-icon--zone {
    width: 14px;
    height: calc(var(--track-h) - 8px);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .stage-icon--nginx {
    border-width: 2.5px;
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--accent-dim);
  }

  /* Client: three small dots instead of a ring — "you", not a server. */
  /* The client keeps its three-dot glyph but wears the same bead as every
     other stage: since the connectors became 2px coloured wires drawn behind
     the strip, a borderless icon let the wire run BETWEEN the dots, which
     read as a broken glyph rather than as a device sending a request. The
     circle's own background masks the wire, exactly as it does for the rest. */
  .stage-icon--client {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .stage-icon--client span {
    width: 3px;
    height: 3px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .stage-label {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere; /* see this block's header comment */
    hyphens: auto; /* "destination" breaks as "desti-nation", not "destinatio-n", if it still has to wrap at all */
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    line-height: 1.15;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .stage--nginx .stage-label {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
  }

  /* ===== Node cluster ==========================================================
     The pool of available nodes, all of them, always visible, never colored
     by which bubble is showing — bubble 4 has the traveling highlight box
     grow to cover the whole cluster (see "Traveling highlight box" and
     `box-travel` above), never a single badge. Numbers/countries are real
     HTML text, not SVG: this is real data from `topology.ts`, not
     decoration. */
  /* Nodes stack: one circle per instance, spread over the shared track so each
     one lines up with the curve that reaches it. `justify-content: space-*` is
     deliberately NOT used — the frontmatter computes the same even spread for
     the SVG, and two independent distributions would drift apart. */
  .node-cluster {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-around;
    height: var(--track-h);
  }

  /* The exits: as many as there are distinct egress countries, centred as a
     group. The countries are not named here — see the fan geometry comment in
     the frontmatter. */
  .egress-stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .node-badge {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .node-badge-num {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    border: 1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.55rem;
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
  }



  /* ===== Shared column positions ===============================================
     The six bubble targets, as center-of-column percentages of the strip's
     8.6-unit grid above (1fr client, 1.3fr Cloudflare, 1fr nginx, 3fr nodes,
     1fr egress, 1.3fr destination — every stage now has a bubble). Column
     gap is 2px, imperceptible next to the figure's own width, so ignoring
     it here is fine. Each `--pos` positions that bubble's leader line and
     its box's speech-bubble tail below — one shared value instead of
     repeating the same percentage three times per bubble. */
  .bubble--1 {
    --pos: 5.81%;
  }

  .bubble--2 {
    --pos: 19.19%;
  }

  .bubble--3 {
    --pos: 32.56%;
  }

  .bubble--4 {
    --pos: 55.81%;
  }

  .bubble--5 {
    --pos: 79.07%;
  }

  .bubble--6 {
    --pos: 92.44%;
  }

  /* ===== The fixed-height bubble zone ==========================================
     `height`, not `min-height`: with every `.bubble` absolutely positioned
     (below), the container has no in-flow content to size itself from, so it
     would collapse to 0 without an explicit height. This value is tuned
     against the longest bubble text in EITHER language at 360px wide, the
     narrowest this site supports — see the SDD report for the rendered
     measurements taken across the animation loop. A FIXED reservation,
     never a per-bubble one, is what actually fixes the defect that sank the
     previous attempt: the page must not move as the bubbles cycle. */
  .mcp-bubbles {
    position: relative;
    height: 5rem;
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .bubble {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0%;
  }

  .bubble--1 {
    animation: phase-1 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--2 {
    animation: phase-2 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--3 {
    animation: phase-3 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--4 {
    animation: phase-4 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--5 {
    animation: phase-5 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--6 {
    animation: phase-6 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  /* The thin line that "points at the piece it describes" — from the top of
     this fixed zone (where the strip ends) down to the bubble's tail. */
  .bubble-leader {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--pos);
    width: 1px;
    height: 0.9rem;
    background: var(--color-border);
  }

  /* `display: flex` is the vertical-centering fix: the number badge is
     `1.1rem` tall, which happens to be close to ONE line of the 0.8125rem/
     1.35 body text — so a one-line bubble already looked fine, but a
     two-line bubble did not. Without flex, `.bubble-num` is an inline box
     that sits next to the FIRST line only; the second line wraps back to
     the box's left edge underneath it, reading as the number pinned to the
     top of a taller block instead of centered against it. `align-items:
     center` on the row centers the badge against the text's full height
     (one line or two, EN or ES — see `.bubble-num`/`.bubble-text` below)
     instead of just its first line. */
  .bubble-box {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Speech-bubble tail: two stacked triangles (border color, then background
     color one pixel smaller) fake a 1px outline where the tail meets the
     leader line above. */
  .bubble-box::before,
  .bubble-box::after {
    position: absolute;
    top: -7px;
    left: var(--pos);
    content: "";
    border-right: 7px solid transparent;
    border-left: 7px solid transparent;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  .bubble-box::before {
    border-bottom: 7px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .bubble-box::after {
    top: -6px;
    border-right-width: 6px;
    border-bottom: 6px solid var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border-left-width: 6px;
  }

  .bubble-num {
    display: flex;
    flex: none; /* fixed circle, never shrunk to make room for the text */
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-bg);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
  }

  /* The text half of the same flex row — `min-width: 0` is the standard
     flexbox fix that lets a text item shrink below its own unbroken content
     width and actually wrap, instead of overflowing `.bubble-box`. */
  .bubble-text {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* ===== Bubble cycle ===========================================================
     A 30s loop, shaped by six DWELLS (11% each, 3.3s — one per bubble/
     stage, including "destination" since the 2026-08-23 destination-hop
     update) separated by six TRAVEL windows: five short 3%-wide hops
     between adjacent stages (11–14%, 25–28%, 39–42%, 53–56%, 67–70%) and
     one long 19%-wide RETURN hop (81–100%) that carries the highlight box
     back from "destination" to "client" to restart the loop. These are the
     exact same breakpoints `box-travel` below uses for the highlight box's
     `left`/`width` — this is what keeps a bubble's text and the box
     arriving at its stage in lockstep, with one shared timeline (30s, same
     start, same `infinite`) instead of two clocks that could drift. Adding
     the sixth dwell+hop shrank the other five dwells (14%→11%) and short
     hops (3% stayed 3%, unchanged) to keep the loop at 30s rather than
     stretching it — see this file's header comment, "UPDATE 2026-08-23
     (destination hop)".

     Each bubble fades in/out over a 3%-wide window (matching the short
     forward hops) positioned at the edges of its own dwell — bubble 1 both
     opens the loop (0–11%) AND closes it (fading in over the last 3% of the
     long return hop, 97–100%, so its 100% value already equals its 0%
     value: a genuinely seamless loop, not a hard cut). Bubble 6 mirrors
     that at the other end: it fades out over the FIRST 3% of the return
     hop (81–84%) and then stays invisible for the rest of that long hop —
     the box is still visibly travelling during that stretch, just with no
     bubble narrating it, which reads as a brief beat before the loop
     restarts rather than a jump cut. (This "fade early, stay dark through
     the long hop" role moved from bubble 5 to bubble 6 with the
     destination-hop update — bubble 5 is now a normal middle-of-the-loop
     bubble like 2/3/4, fading in and out over its own two adjacent short
     hops.) */
  @keyframes phase-1 {
    0%,
    11% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    14%,
    97% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    100% {
